# Line-ending and binary policy — S4 task 1 (Q27, Q39, decision register P5).
#
# WHY. core.autocrlf=true with no .gitattributes made git write CRLF into
# every working tree, so a fresh clone could not build (Q27) and 25 tests
# failed on an ordinary `git checkout -- src/` (Q39). This file makes the
# checkout bytes a property of the repository instead of the machine.
#
# TEXT. Every text file is stored LF and checked out LF on every platform.
# At the commit that added this file no tracked text blob contained CR, so
# renormalisation changed no stored bytes; what changes is what checkout
# writes on a CRLF-configured machine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# FIXTURES get the same policy as all text, on purpose. No committed fixture
# carries CR (nothing tests CRLF input from a fixture file), and fixture
# generators run on Windows can emit CRLF — normalising on add keeps a
# regenerated fixture's blob stable instead of flipping every line. A fixture
# that must preserve CR bytes deliberately would need its own `-text` line.

# BINARY. Never converted, never diffed as text. Declared explicitly rather
# than left to auto-detection, so a binary type added later cannot be
# misread as text and corrupted by a line-ending conversion.
*.zip   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.xls   binary
*.docx  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
